    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d914">

                <pb facs="0884" n="570" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg570"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/38</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of the Treasury</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Newton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Treasury Department</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-06">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 6, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Please advise whether any duties devolve upon collectors of customs and other
                    officers of this department to prevent the exportation of so-called contraband
                    of war in the ordinary course of commerce; if so, please give list of articles
                    recognized as contraband.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d914-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">B. R. Newton</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d914-f1" n="2" xml:id="d914-f1-content">
                <p>See the letter of the Acting Secretary of Commerce to the Secretary of State,
                    August 6, 1914, <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg595">p.
                        595</ref>, for instructions of August 5 to the collector of customs at New
                    York regarding the clearance of foreign merchant vessels, containing the
                    following directions: It must be unquestionable that she has no arms or
                    munitions of war aboard.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
